  "script": "My great-grandmother painted watch dials in New Jersey in the 1920s. The paint glowed because it had radium in it. The girls were told to shape their brushes with their lips, to get a fine point. The company said it was safe. It wasn't. Women's jaws crumbled. Some died young. A group of them sued, and in 1928 they settled. Their fight helped workers win the right to sue over what a job does to their body. When people say nobody covers things up, I think about her."
